# Cloudflare Pages reads this file from the root of the deployed output. Astro copies
# `public/` verbatim, so this lands at `dist/_headers` and needs no build step.
#
# It replaces the `[[headers]]` block in `netlify.toml`, which Netlify read and
# Cloudflare never will. The rule is the same one, and the reason is unchanged: every
# filename under `/_astro/` carries a hash of its own contents, so a URL there can
# never mean two things, and a year of `immutable` costs nothing. Without it each
# hashed asset spends a render-blocking round trip being told it has not changed —
# measured at 168ms and 175ms, transferSize 300, a 304 carrying no content.
#
# THE HASHING IS THE PRECONDITION, not a detail. `tests/build-output.test.ts` asserts
# it separately, because the day one asset lands here without a hash this rule pins a
# stale file for a year and nothing else would say so.

/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
